<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dejima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dejima was a small artificial island in the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>'s policy of restricted foreign contact, it was the sole place where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were permitted to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It became Japan's main channel for European goods and knowledge, the basis of 'Dutch learning' (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dejima.jpg" alt="Dejima" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Isaac Titsingh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plattegrond_van_Deshima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dejima, meaning exit island, measured about 120 metres by 75 and was linked to the mainland by a small bridge, guarded on both sides, with a gate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> side. It held houses for about twenty Dutchmen, warehouses and quarters for Japanese officials. The shogun Iemitsu ordered its construction in 1634 to lodge the Portuguese traders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> and prevent the spread of their religion, one of many edicts he issued between 1633 and 1639 to limit contact with other countries. The island was created in 1636 by cutting a canal through a small peninsula. Twenty-five local Japanese families owned the land and received an annual rent from the Dutch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Portuguese traders had developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> from 1570 with the daimyo Omura Sumitada. After a rising of mostly Christian people in the Shimabara-Amakusa region, the Tokugawa government expelled the Portuguese in 1639. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, which had run a post at Hirado since 1609, was moved to Dejima in 1641, after officials used the Christian-era dates on its new warehouses as a pretext to demolish them. From then only Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships could come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and only to Nagasaki.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were closely watched by officials, gatekeepers, night watchmen and about 150 interpreters, all paid by the company. Ships were inspected, religious books and weapons confiscated, and no religious services were allowed. Engelbert Kaempfer compared the island to a prison. Ships were allowed in numbers that fell from an average of seven a year in 1641-1671 to two a year from 1715. After the company's bankruptcy in 1795 the Dutch government took over, and during the Napoleonic Wars Dejima was for a time the only place where the Dutch flag still flew. The chief Dutch officer, the Opperhoofd, was replaced every year and travelled to Edo to pay homage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, yearly until 1790 and every fourth year afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, books and scientific instruments and bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Staff of the post sold more than 10,000 foreign books to the Japanese between the late 18th and early 19th centuries, a basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, Dutch learning. Physicians such as Kaempfer, Carl Peter Thunberg and Philipp Franz von Siebold were sometimes called to high-ranking Japanese patients. Billiards, chocolate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cabbage are among things introduced through the island.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> negotiated their own treaty in 1858 Dejima ceased to be the exclusive trading post, and land reclamation later merged it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. It was designated a national historic site in 1922, and restoration began in 1996, with plans to rebuild 25 buildings in their early 19th-century state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dejima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The maritime restrictions later known as sakoku, set out in edicts of the 1630s, banned Japanese from travelling abroad, expelled the Portuguese and confined the Dutch from 1641 to the artificial island of <b>Dejima</b> in Nagasaki, where Chinese merchants also traded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1633 the policy of sakoku had kept Japan closed, with only the Dutch trading at <b>Dejima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li></ul></section>
